import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "The database",
	description: "The Roc Solid server platform's database: Postgres, or SQLite or Turso inside the server's process, as the app's program says. Queries whose rows decode into the record you annotate, transactions that take jobs with them, migrations built into the executable, notifications, SQL checked by the database's own engine (rocx check --sql), and roc-pg, a client in Roc whose queries are checked when the app is built.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "choose", label: "Postgres or SQLite" },
		{ id: "query", label: "A query" },
		{ id: "rows", label: "Rows, as records" },
		{ id: "write", label: "Writing" },
		{ id: "transactions", label: "Transactions" },
		{ id: "nested", label: "A transaction inside another" },
		{ id: "migrations", label: "Migrations" },
		{ id: "where", label: "Where it can be used" },
		{ id: "notify", label: "Notifications" },
		{ id: "checked", label: "Checking queries: rocx check --sql" },
		{ id: "turso", label: "Turso" },
		{ id: "roc-pg", label: "roc-pg: queries checked at build" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · the database"
				title="One database, and the types you say"
			>
				An app has one database: Postgres, or SQLite inside the server's own process. <code>Db</code>
				runs statements and decodes rows into the record type you annotate. Transactions keep one
				connection for a call; migrations run once each as the server starts. Actors' state and the jobs
				queue live there too.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="choose">Postgres or SQLite</Heading>
			<p>The program picks the database, and the app's SQL is written for it: nothing is translated.</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Sqlite(path)</code></strong>, the default (<code>data/app.db</code> when the
					program names none), is a file inside the server's process, at <code>path</code> from where the
					server starts, made with its directory when missing. There is nothing to set up. It suits an app
					on one server, which is what the platform runs; every example app with a database uses it.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Postgres</code></strong> is found at <code>--database-url</code> (or
					<code>DATABASE_URL</code>). Without one there is no database, and an app with tables doesn't start
					(<a href="#where">below</a>). Choose it for data other programs share, or more than one server's
					disk holds.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Turso(path)</code></strong> is SQLite's SQL on another engine, whose transactions
					can write at the same time (<a href="#turso">below</a>).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Code
				title="main.roc"
				source={"program = App.program({\n\tinit!: |_started| Ok({}),\n\tdatabase: Sqlite(\"data/plate.db\"),\n})"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				<code>--database-url sqlite:/var/lib/plate.db</code> moves a SQLite app's file (onto a volume, or to
				a test's own). A URL for the other kind of database stops the server at start, naming the one the
				app wants.
			</p>
			<p>
				A package names the databases it works with in its
				<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugin</a>'s needs: <code>Database</code> (either),
				<code>Postgres</code> or <code>Sqlite</code>; a mismatch stops the app at start. A package with SQL
				for both asks <code>Db.backend!</code> which to run, and lists a migration for each
				(<code>{"{ name: \"0001_jobs\", sql: lite, on: Sqlite }"}</code>), as the jobs plugin does.
			</p>

			<Heading id="query">A query</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Db.all!</code> runs a statement with its parameters and returns its rows, of the type the
				annotation says. From the platform's test app:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_notes.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_notes.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>Db.one!</code> reads one row. It is <code>NotFound</code> when there is none (an endpoint
				answers 404), and the first in the statement's order when there are several, so say which you mean
				(<code>order by … limit 1</code>). From the tasks example:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_query.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_query.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Parameters are <code>$1</code>, <code>$2</code>… on both databases, each a <code>Db.Value</code>:
				<code>Null</code>, <code>Text</code>, <code>Int</code> or <code>Bool</code>. Postgres receives text
				and reads it as the type its place wants (<code>$1</code> in <code>where id = $1</code> becomes a
				<code>bigint</code>). SQLite receives each as it is (a <code>Bool</code> as 1 or 0), and a number
				column accepts numeric text. Never build a statement from strings: pass values as parameters.
			</p>
			<p>
				On Postgres, <code>Db.array</code> passes a list as one parameter, in Postgres's text form for
				arrays (<code>{"{\"work\",\"to do\",NULL}"}</code>), read as the array of text or numbers its place
				wants: <code>= any($1)</code>, <code>{"@> $1"}</code>, <code>$1::bigint[]</code>,
				<code>unnest($1::text[])</code> (alone, <code>unnest($1)</code> needs the cast). Each text is quoted,
				so a comma, a brace or the word NULL inside stays text. SQLite has no arrays: pass JSON text and read
				it with <code>json_each</code> (<code>where tag in (select value from json_each($1))</code>).
			</p>
			<Code
				title="An array as a parameter"
				source={"tagged : Try(List({ id : I64 }), [DbErr(Str), BadRow(Str)])\ntagged = Db.all!(\"select id from notes where tags @> $1\", [Db.array(tags.map(|tag| Text(tag)))])\n\nDb.run!(\"delete from notes where id = any($1)\", [Db.array(ids.map(|id| Int(id)))])"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>

			<Heading id="rows">Rows, as records</Heading>
			<p>
				The host hands Roc the rows as JSON, each column as the database typed it. <code>Db.all!</code> and
				<code>Db.one!</code> decode each row into the record you annotated, matching fields to columns by
				name. A row that doesn't fit is <code>BadRow</code>, saying why.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Nulls are typed.</strong> Read a column that may be null as <code>Db.Nullable(a)</code>:
					<code>Null</code> or <code>Some(a)</code>. A null where the record reads a plain value is
					<code>BadRow</code>, naming the column.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>From Postgres:</strong> booleans, integers, floats, text, <code>json</code> and
					<code>jsonb</code>, <code>uuid</code>, and timestamps and dates (as text). Other types need a
					cast (<code>::text</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>From SQLite:</strong> values as they are (an integer or a real as a number, text as a
					string), a column declared <code>boolean</code> as a <code>Bool</code>, one declared
					<code>json</code> as its JSON. A boolean computed in a statement (<code>exists (…)</code>) is 0 or
					1, an <code>I64</code>. SQLite has no time type: store milliseconds in an integer
					(<code>Time.now_millis!</code>).
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="write">Writing</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Db.run!</code> runs a statement and returns how many rows it changed. <code>Db.script!</code>
				runs several statements without parameters (separated by <code>;</code>) as one transaction: if one
				fails, none stays.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_insert.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_insert.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="transactions">Transactions</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Db.transaction!</code> runs a function on one connection. Its writes, and the jobs it queues,
				stay only if it returns <code>Ok</code>. An <code>Err</code>, a failing statement, a crash, or the
				call ending with the transaction open rolls all of it back.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_transaction.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_transaction.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Inside, <code>?</code> works on what <code>Db</code>, <code>Job</code> and your own code return,
				with no error mapping. Their errors are open tag unions (<code>Db.run!</code>'s is
				<code>[DbErr(Str), ..others]</code>, <code>Job.enqueue!</code>'s <code>[JobErr(Str), ..others]</code>),
				so the transaction's error is their union: with an app's own <code>Err(Refused)</code>,
				<code>[DbErr(Str), JobErr(Str), Refused]</code>, which the caller matches.
			</p>
			<p>
				A job queued in a transaction is a row in it: it runs only if the transaction commits, and the
				workers wake when it does (<a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>jobs</a>).
			</p>
			<Callout kind="note" title="Keep transactions short on SQLite">
				<p>
					On SQLite, reads go on beside a write, but writes take turns: a transaction holds the write lock
					from its start, so other writes wait until it ends. Keep anything slow (a request to another
					server, a wait) out of it. On Postgres it would hold only the rows it wrote.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<Heading id="nested">A transaction inside another</Heading>
			<p>
				A helper can open its own transaction and still be called inside another: the inner
				<code>Db.transaction!</code> is then a level of the outer one (a savepoint). On <code>Ok</code>, its
				writes stay in the outer transaction, which commits them or not. On <code>Err</code>, or a failing
				statement, only its own writes are undone, with what it notified and the jobs it queued, and the
				error comes back to the outer body, which can handle it and go on:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_nested_transaction.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_nested_transaction.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					An outer <code>Err</code> still undoes everything, inner levels included, and a crash rolls back
					the whole transaction.
				</li>
				<li>Levels nest as deep as you like; a level's writes stay only if every level around it commits.</li>
				<li>
					It works the same on Postgres, SQLite, and Turso in both modes. On Postgres a failing statement
					aborts the transaction until its level rolls back, so an inner body that ignores the error and
					returns <code>Ok</code> still gets it from <code>Db.transaction!</code>, and its level is undone.
				</li>
				<li>
					On Turso's MVCC, a level that loses to another transaction's write isn't run again alone: the
					whole transaction has lost. Its statements fail from then on, and the outermost
					<code>Db.transaction!</code> runs its body again when the error reaches it.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="migrations">Migrations</Heading>
			<p>
				An app's schema is a list of migrations built into its executable. With <code>rocx build</code>,
				put each in a <code>.sql</code> file in <code>migrations/</code> (<code>0001_tables.sql</code>,
				<code>0002_seed.sql</code>…): the build embeds them in name order. A hand-written program lists them
				in <code>program.migrations</code>, each a name and its SQL, imported from a file:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_migrations.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_migrations.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title={Sources.platform_migration.file} source={Sources.platform_migration.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} plain={Bool.True} />
			<p>
				At start, the server applies each migration it hasn't yet, in order, each in its own transaction,
				and records it by name in <code>rocx_migrations</code>: the platform's tables
				(<code>rocx_actors</code>, <code>rocx_actor_timers</code>) first, then the plugins' (the jobs
				plugin's <code>rocx_jobs</code>, as <code>jobs/0001_jobs</code>), then the app's. A failing migration
				stops the server, saying why. Two servers starting at once (a deploy's old and new) take turns, by
				an advisory lock on Postgres or the write lock on SQLite.
			</p>

			<Heading id="where">Where it can be used</Heading>
			<p>
				Anywhere only the server runs: an endpoint, a middleware, a job, an actor, a stream. A component
				also compiles for the browser, so it can't query; it reads through a GET endpoint
				(<code>Endpoint.read!</code>), which the server answers in-process while rendering. Only the server
				runs the handler, so it may query; the browser's copy has just the endpoint's method and URL
				(<a href={"${Paths.calls}#user"}>who is asking</a>).
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Connections.</strong> A statement borrows a connection from a pool (16 for Postgres, 8
					for a SQLite or Turso file); a transaction keeps one until it ends. Statements are prepared once
					per connection, and kept. SQLite's and Turso's calls run on threads of their own, so a call
					waiting on one holds none of Roc's.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Postgres</strong> connections use TLS when the database offers it (or the URL's
					<code>sslmode</code> asks), checked against the web's certificate authorities. In this
					repository, <code>just db</code> runs a Postgres in Docker and prints its URL.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>No database?</strong> A Postgres app with no URL and no tables still runs: each query
					answers that there is no database, and <code>Db.connected!</code> says so up front. One whose app
					or plugins have tables (the jobs plugin's, auth's) doesn't start, saying which have them; under
					<code>--dev</code> or <code>--debug</code> it starts and logs a line.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>SQLite's file</strong> is in WAL mode, with <code>-wal</code> and <code>-shm</code> files
					beside it. Back it up with <code>sqlite3 data/plate.db ".backup copy.db"</code>, or copy it while
					the server is stopped (a Turso file only that way: the server holds it while it runs).
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="notify">Notifications</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Db.notify!(channel, payload)</code> tells whoever listens on <code>channel</code>, when the
				call's transaction commits (never if it, or the level that notified, rolls back), or at once outside
				a transaction. A call listens with <code>Db.listen!(channel)</code>, then gets each notification from
				<code>Live.next!</code> as a message on the topic <code>{"db:<channel>"}</code>
				(<a href={"${Paths.platform_live}#topics"}>topics</a>). On Postgres it is <code>pg_notify</code>, so
				other processes can notify too; on SQLite it stays within the server. Job workers sleep on it until
				a job is queued.
			</p>

			<Heading id="checked">Checking queries: rocx check --sql</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>rocx check --sql</code> checks an app's SQL with the engine it runs on, and its row types with
				the compiler, without running anything. Each string-literal statement at a <code>Db.all!</code>,
				<code>Db.one!</code> or <code>Db.run!</code> is prepared (never run) on a scratch database that the
				app's own server builds from its migrations: a file for SQLite and Turso, or a database on the
				Postgres server <code>DATABASE_URL</code> names, either removed afterwards. It never migrates a database
				nobody named for it: without the <code>CREATEDB</code> privilege, it says to grant it, or to name a
				database it may migrate (<code>--sql-database URL</code>). <code>rocx dev</code> runs the check
				after each build.
			</p>
			<Code
				source={"$ rocx check --sql app\napp/Store.roc:29:15: SQLite refuses it: no such column: bodyy in select bodyy as body from notes at offset 7\napp/Store.roc:33:16: parameter $2 is given, and the statement doesn't use it\napp/Store.roc:50:10: `body` is read as an integer, and notes.body is a TEXT (text)\napp/Store.roc:58:10: `note` is read as a plain value, and notes.note may be null (it has no `not null`): read it as Db.Nullable(…)\napp/Store.roc:66:10: `title` isn't a column the statement returns (it returns id)\nrocx check --sql: 7 statements checked on SQLite (the rows of 5 against their records): 5 problems"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
				plain
			/>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>The SQL.</strong> The engine judges the statement: its syntax, tables, columns and
					functions, Postgres's types, and what Turso doesn't support yet. A call's parameter list must be
					exactly the statement's <code>$1</code> to <code>$n</code>, each used.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The rows.</strong> For a <code>Db.all!</code> or <code>Db.one!</code> whose rows are
					annotated (<code>rows : List(T)</code>, <code>row : T</code>, or the result of the function the
					call is in), it compares the record with the statement's columns: a field the statement doesn't
					return, a type the column's values can't decode into, and a plain field over a column that may be
					null (no <code>not null</code>), which <code>Db.Nullable</code> should read. rocx reads the type
					with the compiler's parser, in a small app beside a copy of yours (<code>packages/dbcheck</code>),
					so aliases, nominal types and packages' types read as the compiler has them.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What it can't check:</strong> SQL built from strings
					(<code>{"\"select \${columns} from notes\""}</code>) or passed in a variable, which
					<code>--verbose</code> counts and lists; and statements on a table the app creates at run time,
					not in a migration, which you mark with <code>Db.unchecked(sql)</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Where to run it:</strong> by hand, in CI, and in <code>just examples</code> for each
					example. <code>--engine turso</code> asks Turso about a SQLite app (or SQLite about a Turso app).
					Describing a statement takes milliseconds; most of a check is the server starting and migrating.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="turso">Turso: SQLite's SQL on another engine</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Turso(path)</code> is <code>Sqlite(path)</code> on
				<a href="https://github.com/tursodatabase/turso">Turso</a>, SQLite rewritten in Rust, also inside the
				server's process. Its SQL and file format are SQLite's, so what this page says of SQLite holds for it,
				including packages' and plugins' SQLite migrations and needs. <code>Db.backend!</code> answers
				<code>Sqlite</code> for both; <code>Db.engine!</code> tells them apart.
			</p>
			<Code
				title="main.roc"
				source={"program = App.program({\n\tinit!: |_started| Ok({}),\n\tdatabase: Turso(\"data/plate.db\"),\n})"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				The URL picks the engine: <code>--database-url turso:data/plate.db</code> runs a SQLite app on Turso,
				and <code>sqlite:</code> a Turso app on SQLite, to compare them under <code>rocx dev</code>. A release
				(<code>rocx build --release</code>, <code>--image</code>) has only the engine its program names, to
				stay small, and refuses another at start.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>What Turso adds: writes at the same time.</strong> Its file is in MVCC mode. A transaction
				(<code>Db.transaction!</code>) begins <code>concurrent</code>, on its own snapshot, and writes beside
				the others. When two write the same row, the second to commit rolls back and its body runs again (up
				to a hundred times; the outermost body when transactions are <a href="#nested">nested</a>), so keep
				what isn't the database's (a fetch, an email) out of a transaction. A write outside a transaction is
				its own concurrent transaction, retried the same way; a schema change (a migration) takes the file
				to itself. Two transactions that read rows and write different ones both commit, where on SQLite the
				second would wait.
			</p>
			<p>What differs:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					In MVCC mode the file is Turso's alone; SQLite refuses to open it.
					<code>--database-url turso:data/plate.db?mvcc=off</code>, run once, gives it back (in that mode
					Turso's writes take turns, as SQLite's do).
				</li>
				<li>
					The server locks a Turso file while it runs (an OS lock), so <code>sqlite3</code> or a test's
					<code>node:sqlite</code> opens it only after the server stops.
				</li>
				<li>
					Turso has no FTS5, so the full-text search in <code>examples/notes</code> and
					<code>examples/plate</code> needs SQLite, and no <code>order by</code> inside an aggregate
					(<code>json_group_array(value order by key)</code>, which <code>examples/board</code> uses).
				</li>
				<li>
					Turso is younger than SQLite (0.8, before its 1.0). Once it has checkpointed rows, deleting many
					of them badly slows what follows until its next checkpoint;
					<code>pragma wal_checkpoint(truncate)</code> after such a delete avoids it, and
					<code>?mvcc=off</code> doesn't have the problem.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<a href={Paths.examples_sqlite_vs_turso}>examples/sqlite-vs-turso</a> times the same work on both, in
				Roc in the server (writes, reads, scans, JSON, eight callers at once); its page has the numbers.
			</p>

			<Heading id="roc-pg">roc-pg: queries checked at build</Heading>
			<p>
				For Postgres, <a href="https://github.com/niclas-ahden/roc-pg">roc-pg</a> is a client written in Roc.
				It speaks Postgres's protocol over the platform's <code>Tcp</code>, signs in with its
				<code>Random</code>, and checks each query against a schema at compile time: a missing column, a
				parameter of the wrong type, or a nullable column read as non-null fails the build. The platform's
				example (<code>server/examples/roc-pg</code>) uses the same <code>schema.sql</code> as its migration,
				so roc-pg checks against what the server applied.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_rocpg.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_rocpg.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Use either, or both. <code>Db</code> has a pool, transactions that take jobs with them, and rows
				decoded into the record you annotate, checked by <code>rocx check --sql</code>. roc-pg has queries
				the compiler checks, and a connection per use (no pool). Checking the example takes under a second
				from cold, an optimized build about six; the platform's tests build it and run it against Postgres.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>The check's blind spots</strong>, besides SQL it can't read (above): a row whose type
					isn't annotated where rocx can read it, a column from the outer side of a
					<code>left join</code> (taken as its table says), and, on SQLite, an expression's type and
					nullability. Those still show when the query runs (<code>BadRow</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Parameters</strong> are four kinds (null, text, integer, boolean) and arrays of them: no
					floats (pass a number as <code>Text</code>, which Postgres reads as the type its place wants) and
					no bytes yet. A row reads an array column through a cast (<code>array_to_json(tags)</code>).
				</li>
				<li><strong>No cursors or streaming:</strong> a query's rows come back whole.</li>
				<li><strong>SQLite</strong> is a file: no in-memory database (the server's connections couldn't share it), and a blob is read through <code>hex(…)</code>.</li>
				<li><strong>Turso</strong>'s sync to Turso's cloud and its own full-text search aren't used yet; in MVCC mode it has no <code>WITHOUT ROWID</code> tables and no <code>REINDEX</code>.</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
